<NewsArticle slug="ladybird-alpha" titles={["Ladybird alpha is out: a new engine you can install", "Ladybird alpha is out"]} sources={sources}>
  <p class="lede">On November 23, 2026 the Ladybird team shipped its first alpha for macOS and Linux. Until now the only way to try the browser was to compile it yourself. Now you download it, and it updates itself.</p>

  <section aria-labelledby="what">
    <h2 id="what">What's new</h2>
    <p class="prose">Ladybird is the browser that isn't based on Chromium, Firefox or Safari. Its engine (LibWeb, with its own LibJS JavaScript engine) was written from scratch, starting as part of the SerenityOS hobby OS. It still uses a few libraries for things like fonts, 2D graphics and video. The first public build is version 2026.47.7.</p>
    <ul class="prose">
      <li><b>Installable builds</b> for macOS and Linux. There's no Windows build yet.</li>
      <li><b>A rolling alpha channel</b> with automatic updates, so you get fixes as soon as they land.</li>
      <li><b>A broken-site button</b> to report pages that don't work, straight from the browser.</li>
      <li><b>Opt-in crash reports</b>: after a crash you can read the report before you decide to send it.</li>
      <li><b>Tools for web developers</b>: WebDriver for automated tests and support for the Firefox DevTools protocol.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="prose">The alpha follows a busy October. Style and layout moved to their own thread, a new JavaScript JIT landed, and a rewritten WebAssembly engine got about 7× faster on the JetStream 3 Wasm benchmark. Ladybird also gained more than 75,000 passing web-platform tests that month, which put it ahead of Safari on that test suite. The team says AI coding agents did much of that work.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="use">
    <h2 id="use">Should you use it?</h2>
    <p class="prose">Try it, but don't switch to it. The team is upfront about this: some sites won't work, it will crash sometimes, and they make no promises about security yet. It even asks security researchers to attack it. Early testers say it feels fast and loads most sites correctly. Simon Willison ran WebAssembly apps and a Python-in-the-browser REPL in it without problems, but he won't sign in to sensitive accounts with it.</p>
    <p class="prose">A sensible setup: keep your main browser for email, banking and anything you're signed in to, and use Ladybird for casual browsing and to report what breaks. If you build websites, test yours in it now, while the team is actively fixing compatibility.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="changed">
    <h2 id="changed">What we changed on browserwall</h2>
    <p class="prose">We updated <a href="/browsers/ladybird">Ladybird's profile</a>: it's now listed as an alpha with installable builds, instead of pre-alpha. We raised its out-of-box experience score from 1/5 to 2/5: you can now install it, and it updates itself. It isn't higher because sites still break and there's no extension support. We'll look at it again after a few weeks of alpha updates. Ladybird still isn't suggested by the quiz, because we leave experimental browsers out of it.</p>
    <p class="prose">Want something stable that isn't Chromium? See <a href="/best/firefox-based-browsers">the best Firefox-based browsers</a>, or <a href="/wall">filter the wall</a> by engine.</p>
  </section>
</NewsArticle>
